                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "4d29225b-144b-3681-b9d5-d73c116d0097",
                                        "entry_id": "e3384978-7c60-3e48-856b-493f4ab5afcc",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The act of restricting, or the state of being restricted."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "10a094f8-9ef7-3304-b6e9-05c6d2f1695e",
                                        "entry_id": "e3384978-7c60-3e48-856b-493f4ab5afcc",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A regulation or limitation that restricts."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ffa5b77b-48df-4059-aaa0-8637c68a32f4",
                                                "sense_id": "10a094f8-9ef7-3304-b6e9-05c6d2f1695e",
                                                "text": "In the year 1819, Sir Robert Peel brought in a bill, and which finally passed both houses, and received the royal assent on the 2nd July, for continuing the restrictions contained in several acts of parliament, on payments in cash by the bank of England, until the 1st day of May, 1823, and to provide for the gradual resumption of cash payments: [...] VIII. After the first day of May, 1822, the bank of England, if they shall think fit, may pay or exchange the lawful coin of the realm, for any of their own notes, payable on demand."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "26c6d7e5-c523-36c1-9fce-dba82335d392",
                                        "entry_id": "e3384978-7c60-3e48-856b-493f4ab5afcc",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The mechanism by which a cell degrades foreign DNA material."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable",
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "cbd527ec-2dc4-4094-990f-adf91199961a",
                                                "sense_id": "26c6d7e5-c523-36c1-9fce-dba82335d392",
                                                "text": "Echinorhagata is likewise supported by typical echinoderid autapomorphies, inclusive the number of trichoscalids that is reduced from 14 to 6; restriction of lateral accessory spines to females only; and an apparent restriction of the middorsal spines to appear only on segments 4 to 8."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
